         <dc:description>
            <rdf:Alt>
               <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Recommendations for Action&#xD;&#xA;1.&#x9;The draft EUSA should be reconsidered. Article 114 TFEU weakens national authorisation and supervisory procedures; the full harmonisation of national licensing should be critically reassessed.&#xD;&#xA;2.&#x9;A differentiated approach to various market actors is needed. Start-ups, SMEs, and large industrial enterprises have different requirements and should be subject to tailored rules.&#xD;&#xA;3.&#x9;Existing procedures must be urgently simplified. Collective licences should be possible, clear deadlines established, and bureaucratic obstacles systematically reduced to ensure legal certainty and create investment incentives.&#xD;&#xA;4.&#x9;The technical requirements in the EUSA should be proportionate, risk-based, and technology-neutral. Blanket overregulation would stifle innovation and overwhelm smaller actors.&#xD;&#xA;5.&#x9;ESA must continue to play a central role in the management, standard-setting, and strategic coordination of European space programmes. The industry rejects any shift of these functions to EUSPA.&#xD;&#xA;6.&#x9;Internationally established standards ‚Äì such as ISO 24113 ‚Äì should be explicitly incorporated into the EUSA to avoid double regulation and to ensure compatibility with global requirements. The European space industry must not be placed at a competitive disadvantage in the global market.&#xD;&#xA;7.&#x9;The scope of application of the draft should be clearly defined to prevent competitive disadvantages for European launchers.</rdf:li>
            </rdf:Alt>
         </dc:description>
         <xmp:CreateDate>2025-08-26T13:46:23+04:00</xmp:CreateDate>
         <xmp:MetadataDate>2025-08-26T13:46:26+04:00</xmp:MetadataDate>
